go.yaml files bufio.{NewScanner,NewReader} as ipc_recv on the note "When
wrapping os.Stdin" -- a condition no catalogue row can enforce, because the
row sees the CALLEE and the answer is in the ARGUMENT. ipc_recv is in
AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP, so bufio.NewScanner(strings.NewReader(s)) invented an
untrusted_input source out of a caller's string and reported it reaching
exec.Command at confidence: PRECISE. Confidence measures the walk, not the
catalogue, so a precise verdict over a wrong source is the worst-shaped false
positive the tool can emit.

SIZED FIRST, as the item required. On hypergumbo's own reaching-def solver,
the shipped dataflow resolves 83 of 163 bare-local sites -- 50.9% pooled,
25-91% per repo. The item feared a low number would make the work "much
bigger than it looks"; it is not low. Of the 83 resolved, 63 wrap an os.Open
handle (fs_read, not a taint source), 3 an HTTP body, 1 a buffer, and ZERO
wrap os.Stdin. The row's stated condition holds nowhere.

ONE PREDICATE, TWO DIRECTIONS. A /dev/null write and an in-memory read are
the same claim -- this call site touches nothing outside the process -- so
io_target_kind gains `in_memory` and _DISCARDING_TARGET_KINDS becomes
_NON_CROSSING_TARGET_KINDS ("discarding" is write-flavoured and would have
smuggled a second meaning under one name). _source_call_can_mint_taint
mirrors _sink_call_can_carry_taint and both read ONE vocabulary, with a test
that widens it at its single home and asserts both arms follow.

ONLY THE PROVABLE CASES ARE STAMPED. A bare local -- 64.8% of the population
-- stamps nothing, because its boundary is the origin of a variable and that
is a dataflow question. INV-zumin forbids answering it by emitting both
boundaries, so the abstention stays an abstention.

THE REMOVAL IS NOT A CLEAN WIN, AND THIS SAYS SO. Paired A/B, gate disarmed
vs armed, eight arms cold: beads 1974 -> 1957 findings, cilium 623 -> 612,
caddy and jaeger unchanged. All SEVEN removed rows read back against source:
every one wraps a buffer filled from a subprocess's stdout. The item calls
this "pure false-positive removal"; on this corpus it is not. The row is
wrong about WHERE the crossing happens, but the flow tracked real external
data one hop earlier. Net recall reduction until INV-lozat is closed.

The wrong row still went, on the precedent already ruled for io_lib: keeping
a misattributing row because it co-occurs with a real crossing makes the
correct fix look like a regression later.

